## Why the data needed reconciling

Air fryer manufacturers do not agree with each other on how to convert an oven recipe, and I found that some of them do not even agree with themselves. Three separate Ninja instruction manuals (AF100UK, AF300UK, AF400UK) all say to reduce the oven temperature by 10°C. Ninja's own cooking-times webpage gives a different rule for packaged food instead: cut the time by 25% rather than changing the temperature. Instant's official UK conversion guide takes a third approach: reduce the temperature by 20°C and cut the time by 20 to 25% for fresh food, or keep the temperature the same and cut the time by about 50% for frozen food.

None of those three rules is wrong on its own. They answer slightly different questions (a homemade oven recipe, packaged food, fresh versus frozen), but I could not find a UK source that set the actual manual wording side by side so a reader could check where each figure comes from. That is what [thefryerguide.com](https://thefryerguide.com) does: every food page states which manufacturer manual or official guide a cooking time or conversion figure is sourced from, and the [data page](https://thefryerguide.com/data/) lists all of them as open data under a CC BY 4.0 licence so anyone can check the source themselves.

## What is actually on the site right now

I launched fourteen food pages on 27 September 2026, covering fourteen everyday air fryer foods (chicken breast, chicken thighs, chicken wings, salmon, steak, pork chops, sausages, bacon, burgers, roast potatoes, jacket potato, chips, fish fingers and whole chicken), plus a converter page for turning an oven recipe into air fryer settings. The data page currently logs 105 manufacturer-sourced cooking and conversion figures across those pages, compared against five UK air fryer brands' manuals and official guidance: Ninja, Tower, Cosori, Salter and Instant.

This is a newly launched hub, not a traffic or revenue case study. There is no ranking position, click data or affiliate income to report yet. What exists today is the reconciled dataset itself, published openly so other sites, journalists or researchers looking into air fryer cooking times have a sourced UK reference to check against.
